QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Definitions ---------------- */
{id:"L13-001", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"tell",
 concept:"toxin-vs-toxicant", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: define key terms (toxin vs toxicant)",
 stem:"Coral snake venom and the fumes from a factory smokestack are, in the terms toxicologists use, respectively a:",
 options:[
  {t:"Toxin and a toxicant", correct:true, why:"A toxin is a toxic substance produced by a biological system (snake venom); a toxicant is produced by, or is a by-product of, human activity (smokestack emissions)."},
  {t:"Toxicant and a toxin", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two: biological origin makes the venom a toxin; the man-made emission is the toxicant."},
  {t:"Toxin and a toxin", correct:false, why:"The venom is a toxin, but whatever comes out of a factory smokestack is made through human activity, which makes it a toxicant; toxin is kept for substances from biological systems."},
  {t:"Poison and a xenobiotic", correct:false, why:"Both words apply loosely to both substances: poison is the general term for anything that causes a harmful response, and a xenobiotic is any compound foreign to the organism. Neither says where the substance came from; toxin versus toxicant does."}],
 teach:"Poison is the general lay term for any agent that can cause a deleterious response in a biological system. Toxin and toxicant both name noxious substances, but a toxin comes from a biological system (venom) and a toxicant from human activity (industrial emissions). A xenobiotic is any compound in an organism that the organism does not normally make or expect: it may be a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication.",
 quote:"toxins are substances coming from biological systems, so. Snake venom is a toxin, OK? ... A toxicant is made by man through our activities.",
 note:"She added: \"I will often not use those words properly, OK? That's OK. I just wanted you guys to know that ... those two words are out there and they're different.\"",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Definitions’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-002", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"recall",
 concept:"xenobiotic", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: define key terms (xenobiotic)",
 stem:"Which statement about a xenobiotic is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is not normally produced by or expected in the organism", correct:true, why:"That is the definition; a xenobiotic may be a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication."},
  {t:"It is a toxic substance produced by a biological system", correct:false, why:"That is the definition of a toxin, such as snake venom; a xenobiotic is defined by being foreign to the organism, whatever its origin."},
  {t:"It is a toxic substance produced by human activity", correct:false, why:"That is the definition of a toxicant, such as a smokestack emission; a xenobiotic can also be a natural toxin, a food additive or a medication."},
  {t:"It is any agent that causes a deleterious response", correct:false, why:"That is the definition of a poison; a xenobiotic is defined by being foreign to the organism, not by being harmful (a medication is a xenobiotic)."}],
 teach:"A xenobiotic is defined only by being foreign to the organism: a compound that gets in but is not normally produced by it or expected to be present. The word says nothing about origin or harm. That is why it covers a snake's toxin, a man-made toxicant, a food additive or a medication, while toxin and toxicant are defined by where the substance comes from and poison by its ability to cause a bad response.",
 quote:"It just means any sort of compound that gets into an organism that's not normally produced by that organism or expected to be present in that organism",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Definitions’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- The five principles ---------------- */
{id:"L13-003", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"five-principles", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: list the five principles of toxicology",
 stem:"Which of the following are among the five principles of toxicology? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Exposure", correct:true, why:"One of the five: how people or critters are exposed (route, duration, single versus repeated, frequency)."},
  {t:"ADMET", correct:true, why:"One of the five: absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, with a T added for toxicity; the metabolism part includes bioactivation."},
  {t:"Route of exposure", correct:false, why:"Route is one factor within the exposure principle, not a principle of its own."},
  {t:"Target and mechanism of action", correct:true, why:"One of the five: where in the body the substance acts and how it causes harm."},
  {t:"Therapeutic index", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index is a value calculated within the dose principle, not one of the five principles."}],
 teach:"Each principle asks one question about a poison. Source: where does it come from? Exposure: how are people or critters exposed (route, duration, single versus repeated, frequency)? ADMET: what are its kinetics, with a T added for toxicity? Dose: is the exposure high enough to cause a problem? Target and mechanism of action: where does it go in the body, and how does it cause harm? Route, half-life and the therapeutic index are pieces inside a principle, not principles of their own.",
 quote:"We think about the source of a toxin or toxicant. We think about how are people or critters exposed. We have to think about ADM ... we're gonna add the T ... Another principle is dose ... And then the last principle is we think about target and mechanism of action",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘5 Principles of Toxicology’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-004", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"recall",
 concept:"admet-t", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: what ADME stands for",
 stem:"In the toxicology version of ADME, written ADMET, the added T stands for:",
 options:[
  {t:"Target", correct:false, why:"Target and mechanism of action is a separate principle; the T added to ADME is toxicity."},
  {t:"Toxicity", correct:true, why:"Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity."},
  {t:"Threshold", correct:false, why:"Threshold belongs to the dose–response discussion under the dose principle."},
  {t:"Transport", correct:false, why:"The four letters before the T are absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion; the added letter names the outcome toxicologists care about, toxicity, not another kinetic step."}],
 teach:"ADME is the kinetics of anything that gets into the body: it is absorbed, distributed, likely metabolized, then excreted. In the toxicology module the same steps are followed with a T for toxicity added, because the question is whether those kinetics end in harm. The step toxicologists watch most is metabolism, where a compound can be made into something even nastier (bioactivation). Target, threshold and time belong to other principles.",
 quote:"We're in the toxicology module here, so we're gonna add the T to add me, OK? So absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, and toxicity",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘5 Principles of Toxicology’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-005", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"princ", skill:"apply",
 concept:"toxic-target-differs", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: toxicity mechanism vs efficacy mechanism (False)",
 stem:"Which statement about a medication's toxic effect and its therapeutic effect is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The toxic target is the same as the therapeutic target", correct:false, why:"Not necessarily: acetaminophen is taken for soreness, but its toxicity comes from NAPQI acting on the liver. The toxic target can differ from the therapeutic one."},
  {t:"The mechanism of action is the same at each target", correct:false, why:"The target-and-mechanism slide says the mechanism of action at multiple targets may differ."},
  {t:"The toxic target may differ from the therapeutic target", correct:true, why:"The target of a toxic effect is not always the therapeutic target: acetaminophen is taken for soreness, but its toxicity comes from NAPQI acting on the liver."},
  {t:"Metabolism does not contribute to the toxic effect", correct:false, why:"Metabolism can create the toxic species: CYP2E1 bioactivates acetaminophen to NAPQI."}],
 teach:"The target-and-mechanism principle asks where a substance goes in the body and how it causes harm. For a medication that need not be where it works: acetaminophen is taken for soreness, yet its harm comes from NAPQI, made by CYP2E1, which is toxic to the liver. A substance can have several targets, with different toxicity and a different mechanism at each, and the dose reaching each target decides whether toxicity happens there.",
 quote:"sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target, OK? We'll talk about that a little bit more later.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Source ---------------- */
{id:"L13-006", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"source", skill:"apply",
 concept:"source-categories", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: describe general sources of exposure",
 stem:"A pharmacist is exposed to chemotherapy while mixing it in a hood with the wrong airflow. Which source category does this exposure belong to?",
 options:[
  {t:"Environmental", correct:false, why:"Environmental exposure is pollution in the surroundings, such as diesel fumes from a passing bus or dust from a landfill."},
  {t:"Occupational", correct:true, why:"A workplace exposure; healthcare workers can be exposed to chemotherapy, radiation, or a contaminant on a patient brought to the emergency room."},
  {t:"Medications", correct:false, why:"As a source category, medications means a patient receiving the drug. The pharmacist is not taking it; the exposure comes from the job."},
  {t:"Critter bites and stings", correct:false, why:"This source covers venoms and toxins from animals, such as snakes; no animal is involved here."}],
 teach:"Source asks where the exposure comes from, and the category follows the setting of the exposure, not the substance. Chemotherapy is a medication, but a pharmacist breathing it from a hood with the wrong airflow is exposed through the job, so it is occupational, like radiation or a patient splattered with an organophosphate in the ER. Environmental exposures come from the surroundings (diesel fumes, landfill dust). Pathogens such as needle sticks are outside this course.",
 quote:"do you have a risk of occupational exposures as a healthcare provider? ... you could be exposed to um chemotherapy, you know, uh, mixing it up in a in in an in an incorrect hood with the wrong airflow ... Remember, in this course we're not usually thinking about exposure to pathogens like needle sticks",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Source’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: route ---------------- */
{id:"L13-007", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"recall",
 concept:"route-effectiveness-order", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: significance of route",
 stem:"Ranked from most to least effective for delivering a toxic agent, the routes of exposure are:",
 options:[
  {t:"Intravenous, inhalation, oral, dermal", correct:true, why:"The descending order of effectiveness. IV comes first (\"if it's a liquid and you can inject it, IV is the most effective route\") and it skips first-pass metabolism; dermal is last."},
  {t:"Oral, intravenous, inhalation, dermal", correct:false, why:"Oral is the most common route of exposure, not the most effective."},
  {t:"Inhalation, intravenous, dermal, oral", correct:false, why:"Intravenous ranks first and dermal last."},
  {t:"Dermal, oral, inhalation, intravenous", correct:false, why:"This reads the list from least to most effective. Dermal is the weakest route for delivering a toxic agent and IV the strongest, so a most-to-least ranking starts with IV and ends with dermal."}],
 teach:"Two rankings are easy to mix up. Oral is the most common route of exposure, but effectiveness asks how well a route delivers the agent: IV is most effective, then inhalation, oral and dermal. Her route example shows one reason oral sits below IV: an oral dose goes to the liver first (first-pass effect), while an IV dose does not. Healthcare sees more parenteral exposure than other settings, through injected medications and drug abuse.",
 quote:"IV is gonna be your most effective route. If you can give it by IV, if it's a liquid and you can inject it, IV is the most effective route, and then you can see inhalation, oral, and dermal, uh, they decrease in, in the effectiveness",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-008", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"apply",
 concept:"route-first-pass-toxicity", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: significance of route (route affects toxicity of an agent)",
 stem:"A compound can be given orally or by IV, and the liver metabolizes it to a less toxic product. At equal doses, which statement is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The IV dose is less toxic than the oral dose", correct:false, why:"This is reversed: an IV dose skips first-pass metabolism, so the whole dose reaches the body in its more toxic form."},
  {t:"The oral dose is less toxic than the IV dose", correct:true, why:"An oral dose passes through the liver first; if the liver makes a less toxic product, the oral route protects. Her words: the oral route is the least scary one."},
  {t:"The two doses are equally toxic", correct:false, why:"Route changes toxicity whenever first-pass metabolism changes the compound, so equal doses by the two routes differ."},
  {t:"The oral dose skips first-pass metabolism", correct:false, why:"The IV dose is the one that skips first pass; the oral dose goes to the liver first."}],
 teach:"Route affects toxicity when the same compound can enter by more than one route. An oral dose undergoes first-pass metabolism in the liver; if the liver converts the compound into something less toxic, the oral route is the least scary one, while an intravenous dose avoids that protection.",
 quote:"If you had equivalent doses, oral versus IV, and this compound goes through first pass effect. Which is the least scary route of administration. The oral is right, it'll help protect you",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-009", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"route", skill:"apply",
 concept:"formulation-overdose", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient swallows a handful of extended-release β-blocker tablets. Which statement is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The tablets release the drug faster than immediate release", correct:false, why:"Immediate release is the form that absorbs quickly; the extended-release problem is a delayed release all at once."},
  {t:"The tablets can clump in the stomach and then release at once", correct:true, why:"Her answer to immediate- versus extended-release was \"it depends\": immediate release absorbs quickly; extended-release tablets can clump in the stomach and then release the drug all at once."},
  {t:"The vehicle, not the formulation, decides the toxicity", correct:false, why:"Both the vehicle (oily versus aqueous) and the formulation (immediate versus extended release) change toxicity."},
  {t:"The formulation does not change the toxicity of an overdose", correct:false, why:"Formulation and vehicle are two of the factors that change the toxic response to a route of exposure."}],
 teach:"Vehicle and formulation change the toxicity of an exposure. An oily vehicle behaves differently from an aqueous one, and immediate-release and extended-release forms of the same drug differ in overdose: immediate release absorbs quickly, while extended-release tablets can clump in the stomach and release later all at once.",
 quote:"If somebody were to take a handful of an immediate release beta blocker, compared to taking a handful of extended release beta blockers, which do you think is worse? It depends ... the extended release. Oftentimes those compounds, they will, those tablets will clump together in a person's stomach. And then all of a sudden it gets released",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Route’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: duration, single vs repeated ---------------- */
{id:"L13-010", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"recall", type:"match",
 concept:"exposure-durations", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: chronic exposure is more than 3 months",
 stem:"Match each duration of exposure used in animal studies with its definition.",
 left:["Acute", "Subacute", "Subchronic", "Chronic"],
 right:["Less than 24 hours", "Less than 1 month", "1 to 3 months", "More than 3 months, up to years"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Acute", r:"Less than 24 hours", why:"A single day or less."},
  {l:"Subacute", r:"Less than 1 month", why:"Repeated exposure for up to a month."},
  {l:"Subchronic", r:"1 to 3 months", why:"One to three months of repeated exposure."},
  {l:"Chronic", r:"More than 3 months, up to years", why:"Three months or longer."}],
 teach:"Animal exposure studies define the duration of exposure: acute is less than 24 hours, subacute less than one month, subchronic one to three months, and chronic more than three months up to years. Humans are exposed on the same scales, but a person often cannot say how long a workplace or environmental exposure has been going on.",
 quote:"acute is for exposure for less than 24 hours. For for animal studies, for subacute, it's less than 1 month. Sub-chronic is 1 to 3 months, and then chronic is more than 3 months up to years",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Duration’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-011", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"apply",
 concept:"single-vs-repeated-benzene", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: single versus repeated exposure",
 stem:"One walk into a building with a small benzene leak gives a headache; years of daily exposure to the same leak cause leukemia. This illustrates that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Tolerance to benzene develops with repeated exposure", correct:false, why:"The daily worker still gets the headache every day; no tolerance develops."},
  {t:"The route of exposure differs between the two cases", correct:false, why:"Both exposures are the same inhaled gas; what differs is single versus repeated exposure."},
  {t:"A single and a repeated exposure can differ in effect", correct:true, why:"Benzene is her example: one exposure gives headache and drowsiness; chronic low-dose exposure is carcinogenic and causes leukemias."},
  {t:"Leukemia is an immediate effect of benzene exposure", correct:false, why:"Leukemia is the delayed effect of chronic low-dose exposure; the immediate effect is headache or sleepiness."}],
 teach:"Repeating an exposure can change the kind of effect, not just its size. Benzene is a gas workers cannot smell or see, so a small leak exposes them without their knowing. One walk-in gives a headache or sleepiness; going in every day delivers chronic low-dose exposure, and benzene is a carcinogen that causes leukemias. So the effect of a single exposure does not predict the effect of repeated exposure.",
 quote:"anybody know what the dangerous. Health effect. Uh, that can occur with benzene exposure, chronic low dose benzene exposure ... it's a carcinogen, huh, causes leukemias, uh, so wow, getting sleepy and a headache is really different than, you know, like getting leukemia",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-012", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"apply",
 concept:"delayed-effect-des", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: single versus repeated exposure (DES, also her delayed-effect example on 10/13)",
 stem:"Women given diethylstilbestrol (DES) during pregnancy had little more than nausea; their daughters developed a vaginal cancer about 20 years later. This is an example of:",
 options:[
  {t:"A delayed effect of an exposure", correct:true, why:"The exposure was in utero; the effect appeared about twenty years later, which took epidemiology to uncover."},
  {t:"An immediate effect of an exposure", correct:false, why:"An immediate effect occurs rapidly after a single administration; the mothers' nausea was the immediate effect, and the cancer came decades later."},
  {t:"Tolerance after repeated exposure", correct:false, why:"Tolerance concerns whether a repeated exposure keeps producing its effect (her benzene worker developed none). DES harm appeared about twenty years after the exposure: a delayed effect."},
  {t:"A hormetic effect at a low dose", correct:false, why:"Hormesis is a protective low-dose effect of a non-essential substance; this is a harmful delayed one."}],
 teach:"The immediate effect of an exposure does not show everything it can do. Mothers given DES, an estrogen used in the 1950s to help keep a pregnancy, had little more than nausea. Their daughters were exposed in utero, were born and grew up, and in their twenties developed a very unusual vaginal cancer. Because the harm appeared about twenty years later and in a different person, it took a lot of medical epidemiology work to link it to DES.",
 quote:"We started seeing a very weird and scary phenomenon about 20 years after these women were given DES ... the female children of these women ... would develop a very certain kind of vaginal cancer",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-013", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"duration", skill:"recall",
 concept:"chronic-immediate-each-time", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The worker exposed to the unrepaired benzene leak feels sleepy with a headache every single day he goes in. Which point about exposure does this show?",
 options:[
  {t:"Chronic exposure gives delayed effects, not immediate ones", correct:false, why:"The immediate effect keeps happening with every exposure; chronic exposure can give both kinds."},
  {t:"Tolerance develops to the immediate effect over time", correct:false, why:"In this example no tolerance developed: the headache returns every day."},
  {t:"Chronic exposure can give an immediate effect after each exposure", correct:true, why:"The third point on single versus repeated exposure: repeated exposure does not stop each exposure from producing its immediate effect; no tolerance developed."},
  {t:"The headache comes from benzene building up in the blood", correct:false, why:"Build-up in the blood is the frequency figure's point. Here the headache returns because each day is a new exposure that produces its own immediate effect."}],
 teach:"Repeated exposure does not switch off the immediate effect. Because the leak is never fixed, each day's walk-in is a new exposure, and each one brings the same short-term headache and sleepiness; the worker develops no tolerance. So a chronic exposure can carry both kinds of effect: the immediate one after every exposure and, for benzene, the delayed risk of leukemia from low-dose exposure over time.",
 quote:"chronic exposure may produce immediate effects after each exposure ... Every time I walk into my workplace, I just get sleepy and a little bit of a headache ... they're not developing any tolerance",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Single vs. Repeated’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Exposure: frequency and half-life ---------------- */
{id:"L13-014", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"fig2-4-halflife", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: frequency of exposure (half-life figure)", simg:"smith-fig2-4", simgAlt:"Casarett and Doull Figure 2-4: concentration at the target site over time for compounds A, B and C, after one large dose (left) and after repeated smaller doses (right), with the toxic range shaded.",
 stem:"In this figure, which compound has the longest half-life?",
 options:[
  {t:"Compound A", correct:true, why:"The slowest fall in blood concentration means the longest time for half of the compound to be cleared (caption: half-life about 1 year)."},
  {t:"Compound B", correct:false, why:"B clears faster than A; its half-life is about equal to the dosing frequency (caption: about 1 day)."},
  {t:"Compound C", correct:false, why:"C clears fastest, so it has the shortest half-life (caption: about 5 hours)."},
  {t:"Compounds A and B equally", correct:false, why:"A's decline is flatter than B's: the caption gives A a half-life of about a year and B about a day."}],
 teach:"Half-life is the time for half the compound to be cleared from the blood, so after one dose a flatter decline means a longer half-life. In Figure 2-4, A barely slopes down (caption: half-life about 1 year), B falls faster (about 1 day) and C drops steeply (about 5 hours) because it is metabolized and cleared quickly. So A has the longest half-life and C the shortest.",
 quote:"Which compound has the longest half-life in this image? ... A, so it's half-life is longest, right? It takes longer for half the compound to be cleared out of the blood, OK? C has the shortest half-life.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’ (Figure 2-4 and its caption); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-015", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"fig2-4-repeated", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: frequency of exposure", simg:"smith-fig2-4", simgAlt:"Casarett and Doull Figure 2-4: concentration at the target site over time for compounds A, B and C, after one large dose (left) and after repeated smaller doses (right), with the toxic range shaded.",
 stem:"In the repeated-dose panel, which compound never reaches the toxic concentration?",
 options:[
  {t:"Compound A", correct:false, why:"A has the longest half-life and reaches the toxic zone by about the third dose."},
  {t:"Compound B", correct:false, why:"B accumulates and enters the toxic zone by about the fourth or fifth dose."},
  {t:"Compounds B and C", correct:false, why:"B does reach the toxic zone, at about the fourth or fifth dose; only C stays below it."},
  {t:"Compound C", correct:true, why:"C's short half-life clears each dose before the next, so it never accumulates into the toxic zone at that dose and frequency."}],
 teach:"With repeated quarter doses, a compound accumulates when its half-life is long compared with the dosing interval. A reaches the toxic zone by about the third dose and B by about the fourth or fifth, but C, cleared between doses, never does. For a medication, the dosing frequency is chosen so that the concentration stays below toxicity and, ideally, within the therapeutic range, like C.",
 quote:"How about C? It's got a, it's got a really short half-life. It's clearing really quickly. Does it ever get into the toxic zone after repeated exposures? No, it doesn't, right?",
 note:"In one sentence she said the body clears a compound between spaced exposures \"because the half-life is long\"; her reading of the figure, and the keyed answer, is the reverse: \"C has the shortest half-life. It gets metabolized and cleared really quickly\".",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-016", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"adverse-despite-low-concentration", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: a compound cleared between exposures may still cause chronic adverse effects (True)",
 stem:"Compound C stays below the toxic blood concentration at its dose and frequency. Which could still produce a bad response to it? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"An allergic reaction", correct:true, why:"An allergy needs very little drug and has nothing to do with the blood concentration."},
  {t:"Toxicity at a different target site", correct:true, why:"The curve shows one target; the compound may act elsewhere."},
  {t:"Accumulation into the toxic range", correct:false, why:"C does not accumulate; that is the point of its short half-life. A and B are the compounds that build up."},
  {t:"Synergy with another drug", correct:true, why:"She said C 'could synergize with other drugs' and give 'additive adverse effects'. In the 10/13 terms these are two kinds of interaction (additive: the sum of the effects; synergistic: much more than the sum); either can cause harm while C alone stays below its toxic blood concentration."},
  {t:"Damage at the injection site", correct:true, why:"Irritation or damage at a subcutaneous or intradermal injection site is an adverse response unrelated to kinetics."}],
 teach:"A compound that is kept below its toxic blood concentration can still cause harm by routes that have nothing to do with kinetics: an allergy, action at a different target, synergy with another drug, or irritation at the injection site. The healthcare provider still has to watch for these.",
 quote:"Could you still have a bad response to it? Yeah ... you could be allergic to it ... Might be going to a different target site ... Could synergize with other drugs ... you inject the drug, you get a therapeutic response to it, but you're causing damage at the injection site",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- ADMET: bioactivation ---------------- */
{id:"L13-017", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"tell",
 concept:"bioactivation-vs-detoxification", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: significance of bioactivation",
 stem:"Metabolism that turns a compound into something more harmful than the parent is called:",
 options:[
  {t:"Detoxification", correct:false, why:"Detoxification is biotransformation that eliminates the toxicant or prevents its formation: metabolism to something less harmful."},
  {t:"First-pass effect", correct:false, why:"First pass is where metabolism happens after an oral dose, not what the metabolism produces."},
  {t:"Bioactivation", correct:true, why:"Biotransformation of a potential poison to an active, harmful form; her preferred word is bioactivation (also toxication or metabolic activation)."},
  {t:"Conjugation", correct:false, why:"Conjugation to a glucuronide or a sulfate is the phase 2 metabolism of a normal acetaminophen dose, which is detoxification."}],
 teach:"Within ADMET, the metabolism step can go two ways. Bioactivation (toxication, metabolic activation) is biotransformation to an active, harmful form; detoxification is biotransformation that eliminates the ultimate toxicant or prevents it from forming. Acetaminophen can go either way.",
 quote:"I use the word bioactivation for that. Some people use the word toxication, toxification ... when metabolism occurs to create something that's more noxious than what you were originally exposed to.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘ADME’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-018", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"apply",
 concept:"acetaminophen-napqi", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: significance of bioactivation (acetaminophen example)",
 stem:"When a dose of acetaminophen exceeds what the phase 2 enzymes can handle, which enzyme produces the liver-toxic metabolite NAPQI?",
 options:[
  {t:"A glucuronosyl transferase", correct:false, why:"Glucuronidation is the phase 2 route that detoxifies most of the dose."},
  {t:"A sulfotransferase", correct:false, why:"Sulfation is the other phase 2 route that handles the normal dose."},
  {t:"Glutathione S-transferase", correct:false, why:"With glutathione, the glutathione S-transferases detoxify NAPQI after it forms; they do not make it."},
  {t:"CYP2E1", correct:true, why:"The overflow goes down the phase 1 P450 pathway, specifically CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI."}],
 teach:"Most of a normal daily dose of acetaminophen is handled by two families of phase 2 enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases, giving a glucuronide or a sulfate. When those enzymes cannot keep up, the dose spills into the phase 1 P450 route, CYP2E1, which makes NAPQI, the metabolite that is toxic to the liver. That spill-over is bioactivation.",
 quote:"it goes down through our P450s to do, you know, one of our phase one enzymes, right? Specifically P4452E1. We'll do this work to create that nasty NAQI, OK? And you guys already know that the NAQI metabolite is toxic to the liver",
 note:"In speech she calls the glucuronosyl transferases \"GSTs\" (\"GSTs and STs\"); the slide spells them out as glucuronosyl-transferases and sulfo-transferases. On the same slide the glutathione S-transferases, with glutathione, are the separate step that detoxifies NAPQI; that is the family the abbreviation GST normally names.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘ADME: Metabolism’, ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-019", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"recall",
 concept:"acetaminophen-daily-max", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The total daily dose of acetaminophen that FDA labeling says not to exceed is:",
 options:[
  {t:"2 g", correct:false, why:"Lower than the labeled maximum, though she advises older adults to stay under 4 g."},
  {t:"4 g", correct:true, why:"Four grams in 24 hours is the labeled maximum; she advises her older relatives to stay below it."},
  {t:"6 g", correct:false, why:"Above the 4 g labeled maximum. Past that dose the phase 2 enzymes are less able to handle all the acetaminophen, so more of it goes through CYP2E1 to the liver-toxic NAPQI."},
  {t:"8 g", correct:false, why:"Twice the 4 g labeled maximum; at that dose even people with normal phase 2 enzymes are expected to send part of it through CYP2E1 to NAPQI."}],
 teach:"The FDA labeling sets 4 g as the acetaminophen total in a 24-hour period. Her advice to older adults is to stay under that, because the phase 2 enzymes that detoxify the drug may be fewer with age, so more of the dose spills into CYP2E1 and NAPQI.",
 quote:"Total daily dose of acetaminophen that we should all be sticking to, not taking more of. 4 g, right? That's on the FDA labeling, right?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-020", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"admet", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"napqi-below-4g", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient never exceeds 4 g of acetaminophen a day yet develops liver injury. Which of the following could explain NAPQI forming at that dose? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"A genetic variant that lowers phase 2 enzyme activity", correct:true, why:"With less glucuronosyl transferase or sulfotransferase activity, part of a normal dose is pushed through CYP2E1 to NAPQI; this is pharmacogenomics."},
  {t:"Inhibition of CYP2E1 by alcohol", correct:false, why:"Inhibiting CYP2E1 would mean less NAPQI, not more. Alcohol does the opposite: it induces CYP2E1, so the body makes more of the enzyme and more of a normal dose is bioactivated."},
  {t:"Induction of CYP2E1 by alcohol", correct:true, why:"Alcohol induces CYP2E1, so more of the normal dose becomes NAPQI; her baseball-weekend example."},
  {t:"Increased glucuronosyl transferase activity", correct:false, why:"More phase 2 activity would detoxify more of the dose, not less; her causes were weaker phase 2 enzymes or more CYP2E1."}],
 teach:"Two things can set up liver toxicity at or below the allowed dose: a genetic variant that weakens the phase 2 enzymes, so some of the dose goes through CYP2E1, and induction of CYP2E1 by alcohol, so more NAPQI is made. Both push a normal dose toward bioactivation.",
 quote:"They may have a genetic variation. Of those enzymes that reduces those phase two enzymes activity ... sometimes you can get an increased amount of 20 being made by the body. We call that induction ... Alcohol can induce 20 expression.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Example of Bioactivation’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Dose ---------------- */
{id:"L13-021", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"recall",
 concept:"dose-makes-poison", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"\"The dose makes the poison\" is attributed to:",
 options:[
  {t:"Casarett and Doull", correct:false, why:"They wrote the classic toxicology textbook the LD50 table and figures come from."},
  {t:"Paracelsus", correct:true, why:"The saying is attributed to Paracelsus, who studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago."},
  {t:"Rodriguez-Mozaz", correct:false, why:"Rodriguez-Mozaz is the first author of the assigned article on pharmaceuticals in water."},
  {t:"Hippocrates", correct:false, why:"The saying is credited to Paracelsus, who studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago, not to Hippocrates."}],
 teach:"Dose is its own principle because whether something poisons depends on how much is received, not only on what it is. Ethyl alcohol, which people drink, has a rat LD50 of 10,000 mg/kg yet can still cause alcohol poisoning at the right dose, while botulinum toxin kills at 0.00001 mg/kg. The idea that the dose makes the poison is attributed to Paracelsus, who studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago.",
 quote:"Dose makes the poison. Who said that? Anybody know who said that? Supposed to be attributed to a guy named Paracelsus",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-022", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ld50-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: dose-response curves (LD50)",
 stem:"The LD50 of a compound is the dose that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Kills 50% of the animals that receive it", correct:true, why:"The lethal dose for half of the exposed population, measured in rats for the table she showed."},
  {t:"Gives the desired effect in 50% of the animals", correct:false, why:"That is the ED50, the effective dose."},
  {t:"Gives a toxic effect in 50% of the animals", correct:false, why:"That is the TD50, the toxic dose."},
  {t:"Is the lowest dose that causes an effect", correct:false, why:"That is the threshold dose, the other value read from a population curve."}],
 teach:"LD50 comes from a population dose–response study: animals receive different doses, deaths are grouped by dose, and the dose at which cumulative mortality reaches 50% is the LD50 (about 100 mg/kg in the figure she read). Because it counts deaths, it differs from the ED50 (desired effect) and TD50 (toxic effect) taken at the same 50% point, and from the threshold, the lowest dose with any effect.",
 quote:"this is the lethal dose that will kill off 50% of the animals given this compound",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-023", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ld50-table-range", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Of these compounds, which has the lowest LD50 in rats?",
 options:[
  {t:"Ethyl alcohol", correct:false, why:"The top of the table at 10,000 mg/kg: the largest dose is needed, so it is the least potent poison listed."},
  {t:"Nicotine", correct:false, why:"1 mg/kg: potent, but botulinum toxin is 100,000 times more so."},
  {t:"Botulinum toxin", correct:true, why:"0.00001 mg/kg: the bottom of the table and the most potent poison listed."},
  {t:"Dioxin (TCDD)", correct:false, why:"0.001 mg/kg: second from the bottom; botulinum toxin is 100 times more potent."}],
 teach:"LD50 is the dose that kills half the animals, so the smaller the LD50, the less of the compound it takes to kill and the more potent the poison. The rat table runs from ethyl alcohol at 10,000 mg/kg down to botulinum toxin at 0.00001 mg/kg, nine orders of magnitude. So the dose makes the poison, but the compound sets how small a harmful dose can be: \"sometimes it only takes a tiny bit of something to be very, very harmful.\"",
 quote:"10,000 mgs per kilogram for ethyl alcohol would be the LD50 in rats, and 0.00001 would be the LD50 for botulinum toxin in rats, OK? So the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you're talking about too.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’ (Casarett & Doull Table 2-1); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-024", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"dose", skill:"apply",
 concept:"botulinum-can", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A can of corn squirts when it is opened. Why should it be thrown away rather than cooked?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cooking would concentrate the botulinum toxin in the corn", correct:false, why:"Cooking neither concentrates nor destroys the toxin; the point is that it does not destroy it."},
  {t:"It may hold botulinum toxin, which cooking does not destroy", correct:true, why:"The gas from the multiplying bacteria is what makes the can squirt; the toxin, not the bacteria, is lethal, and heating does not break it down."},
  {t:"Live botulinum bacteria in the corn are what cause the harm", correct:false, why:"Her words: \"it's not the bacteria that, that kills you, it's the botulinum toxin that those bacteria are making\"."},
  {t:"The squirting shows the corn is too acidic to be safe to eat", correct:false, why:"Acidity is not the hazard: acidic foods such as tomatoes are usually fine, and the risk is in low-acid vegetables such as beans, peas and corn."}],
 teach:"Her public-service note on the LD50 table: botulinum bacteria can grow inside a sealed can of low-acid vegetables (not usually tomatoes) because they do not need oxygen; as they multiply they release the toxin and gas, so the can squirts on opening. Cooking does not break down botulinum toxin, so the can is thrown away.",
 quote:"it's not the bacteria that, that kills you, it's the botulinum toxin that those bacteria are making as they're growing in your can of corn, OK? ... you can't heat it, cook it, and break down that botulinum toxin",
 note:"She called the organism \"an aerobic bacteria\" and then said \"it doesn't need oxygen\"; the second statement is the one that matches botulinum growth in a sealed can. A public-service aside, not an objective; she said on 10/19 that everything said in class is testable.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Individual vs population dose–response ---------------- */
{id:"L13-025", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"curves", skill:"tell",
 concept:"individual-vs-population-curve", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Rodents receive graded doses of an organophosphate, and percent inhibition of carboxylesterase and of cholinesterase is plotted against dose. This is a(n):",
 options:[
  {t:"Population dose–response relationship", correct:false, why:"A population curve groups responses (such as deaths) by dose; it gives LD50 and threshold, not enzyme inhibition per individual."},
  {t:"Individual dose–response relationship", correct:true, why:"The response of individuals to graded doses; here two enzymes are inhibited, two targets."},
  {t:"Cumulative mortality curve", correct:false, why:"A cumulative mortality curve is the population plot; no deaths are counted here."},
  {t:"Hormetic dose–response curve", correct:false, why:"A hormetic curve is U-shaped, with a protective effect at a low dose; nothing beneficial is measured here."}],
 teach:"An individual dose–response describes the response of an individual to varying doses (here, graded enzyme inhibition at each dose, sometimes on a log dose axis). Measuring the right things on such a curve reveals that many chemicals have more than one effect because of multiple targets: the organophosphate inhibits both carboxylesterase and cholinesterase. A population dose–response describes how a population responds to varying doses and yields LD50 and threshold.",
 quote:"many chemicals have more than one effect because they have multiple targets. For example, these organophosphate exposures are inhibiting these two different kinds of enzymes. And we could not see this if we weren't graphing it as an individual dose response curve",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘First Dose-Response Relationship’ (C&D Fig 2-5); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-026", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"curves", skill:"figure",
 concept:"population-curve-ld50-threshold", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: interpret dose-response curves (LD50 and threshold)", simg:"smith-fig2-6-pop", simgAlt:"Population dose-response: mortality frequency histogram, cumulative mortality curve, and mortality in probit units against dose (mg/kg).",
 stem:"In this population dose–response figure, how are the LD50 and the threshold dose read on the cumulative mortality curve?",
 options:[
  {t:"LD50 at the first dose with any deaths; threshold at 50% mortality", correct:false, why:"This swaps the two readings: the first dose with an effect is the threshold, and 50% cumulative mortality is the LD50."},
  {t:"LD50 at 50% mortality; threshold at the first dose with an effect", correct:true, why:"In the figure she read, 50% cumulative mortality fell at about 100 mg/kg and the first effect at about 10 mg/kg."},
  {t:"LD50 at 50% mortality; threshold at the peak mortality frequency", correct:false, why:"The LD50 half is right, but the threshold sits at the low-dose end, where effects first appear, not at the peak of the mortality-frequency plot."},
  {t:"LD50 at 100% mortality; threshold at the dose with no effect", correct:false, why:"LD50 is defined at 50% mortality, and the threshold is the first dose with an effect, not a dose with none."}],
 teach:"A population dose–response is plotted as mortality frequency against dose and, more usefully, as cumulative mortality. The LD50 is the dose at 50% cumulative mortality; the threshold is the lowest dose at which an effect first appears. The probit-unit version in the textbook figure is old-fashioned and, in her words, nobody uses it.",
 quote:"this is where 50% of the animals die, and the dose here is about 100 mg per kg. OK. And then the threshold would be showing you. Where is the dose where you first start seeing an effect happening",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Second Dose-Response Relationship’ (C&D Fig 2-6); transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Shapes and threshold ---------------- */
{id:"L13-027", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"tell",
 concept:"u-shape-essential-vs-hormesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: describe different dose-response curves (U-shaped)",
 stem:"Iron and red wine both give U-shaped dose–response curves. Which feature is found on the iron curve but not on the red wine curve?",
 options:[
  {t:"A U-shaped dose–response", correct:false, why:"Both curves are U-shaped; the figure shows two different reasons for the shape."},
  {t:"A region of homeostasis", correct:true, why:"Iron: deficiency (death) → homeostasis → overdose. Red wine is not essential, so its curve has no region of need: a possible protective effect at a low dose, then an adverse effect."},
  {t:"A toxic region at high doses", correct:false, why:"Both have one: iron can be overdosed, and too much red wine gives an adverse effect."},
  {t:"A protective effect at low doses", correct:false, why:"That is the red wine (hormetic) curve; at the lowest doses of iron the response is bad (deficiency)."}],
 teach:"Two U-shaped curves that look alike. For an essential substance such as iron, vitamin A or vitamin D, too little is harmful (deficiency), the middle is the region of homeostasis, and too much is toxic. For a non-essential substance such as red wine, hormesis means a low dose may be protective and a higher dose adverse, with no region of homeostasis. Which one applies depends on what the exposure is.",
 quote:"because this is not an essential nutrient, an essential element that we need, there's really no area of homeostasis ... it's illustrating the example of Homesis",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Shape of Dose-Response’ (C&D Fig 2-7); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-028", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"recall",
 concept:"hormesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Hormesis describes a substance that:",
 options:[
  {t:"Is essential, with deficiency below and toxicity above a range", correct:false, why:"That is the essential-nutrient U-curve (deficiency, homeostasis, toxicity), such as iron, not hormesis."},
  {t:"Has a response from the first molecule, with no threshold", correct:false, why:"A linear no-threshold response is the theory for carcinogens."},
  {t:"Builds up to a toxic level when doses are repeated often", correct:false, why:"Building up with repeated doses depends on half-life and dosing frequency (compounds A and B on the frequency figure); hormesis is about the shape of the response as the dose rises."},
  {t:"Is non-essential, protective at a low dose and harmful when higher", correct:true, why:"Her example is a glass or a glass and a half of red wine as possibly heart protective, then too much."}],
 teach:"Hormesis is the U-shaped response of a non-essential substance: a low dose produces a beneficial or protective effect and a higher dose an adverse one. Because the substance is not needed, there is no region of homeostasis. It is a concept toxicologists use; few others do.",
 quote:"my glass or glass and half of red wine is possibly protective, and then I go too far and I should not be driving. OK? So, again, this is a U-shaped curve.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Shape of Dose-Response’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-029", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"tell",
 concept:"threshold-vs-linear", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: significance of threshold",
 stem:"For which kind of agent is a linear, no-threshold dose–response (a response possible from the first molecule) used as the model?",
 options:[
  {t:"Medications", correct:false, why:"Medications follow the S-shaped curve with a threshold below which there is no effect."},
  {t:"Essential nutrients", correct:false, why:"Essential nutrients give the U-shaped curve with a region of homeostasis."},
  {t:"Carcinogens", correct:true, why:"That is how carcinogens were long modeled: linear from zero, possibly no safe dose."},
  {t:"Hormetic substances", correct:false, why:"Hormesis gives a U-shaped curve, not a linear one."}],
 teach:"An S-shaped dose–response implies a threshold: a dose below which there is no effect, therapeutic or toxic. That is probably the case for medications and most poisons. Carcinogens have been modeled as linear with no threshold, on the theory that a single molecule damaging DNA could start a cancer. Her qualifier: possibly.",
 quote:"That's a theory for exposure to carcinogens. Maybe it just takes one molecule of a carcinogen to cause you problems ... So don't always assume everything has an area where a dose is not harmful.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Threshold Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-030", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cumulative-dose-threshold", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", lowYield:true,
 stem:"In the 2-AAF hamster study, the bladder-tumor threshold was lower at 33 months than at 24 months. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Their daily dose was higher", correct:false, why:"The dosing groups were the same; only the length of dosing differed."},
  {t:"Their cumulative dose was higher", correct:true, why:"Cumulative dose is the keyword a student gave and she confirmed: more months of daily dosing means a higher total dose."},
  {t:"They metabolized 2-AAF faster with age", correct:false, why:"Metabolism was not the explanation; the total dose received was."},
  {t:"Old hamsters get bladder tumors anyway", correct:false, why:"That was true of liver tumors, not bladder tumors, in this study."}],
 teach:"With daily dosing, a longer exposure means a higher cumulative dose, so the threshold for a response falls when animals are observed longer. In the 2-AAF study the bladder-tumor threshold was about 75 ppm at 24 months and about 45 ppm at 33 months. She said she would not put a graph like this on an exam (marked low yield); the point is what cumulative dose does to a threshold.",
 quote:"So the cumulative dose that these animals got was higher at 33 months, right, compared to 24 months ... That's why it didn't take as much of the dose to cause threshold.",
 note:"\"I will not give you a graph like that I never have ... It's very unlikely I would give you this kind of graph on an exam.\"",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘More on Threshold Dose’ (C&D Fig 2-8); transcript 10/9"},

{id:"L13-031", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"shape", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cancer-disease-of-aging", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"In the 2-AAF study, hamsters that received no compound and were sacrificed at 33 months had liver tumors. What does this show?",
 options:[
  {t:"The control animals were dosed by mistake", correct:false, why:"It was a well-done study; the controls truly received no 2-AAF."},
  {t:"Liver tumors have no threshold dose for 2-AAF", correct:false, why:"The tumors in the zero-dose group cannot be caused by a compound they never received; they came with age."},
  {t:"Hamsters are a poor model for liver tumors", correct:true, why:"The zero-dose, 33-month group had a significant number of liver tumors on their own: cancer is a disease of aging, and old hamsters get liver tumors anyway."},
  {t:"The bladder tumors spread to the liver", correct:false, why:"Nothing in the study pointed to spread; the untreated animals had no compound to cause either tumor."}],
 teach:"The second lesson from the 2-AAF figure: the untreated 33-month hamsters had liver tumors, because cancer is a disease of aging and old hamsters develop liver tumors anyway. A species that gets the tumor spontaneously is the wrong model for testing whether a compound causes that tumor.",
 quote:"Is cancer the disease of the aged? Yes, cancer is a disease of the aged, usually ... if we're gonna look and see if 2AAF causes liver cancer, hamsters are not the best animals to use for this, because they get them anyway",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘More on Threshold Dose’; transcript 10/9"},

/* ---------------- Indices and target (slides she did not reach on 10/9) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-032", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-mos-moe", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: TI formula; objective: calculate common indices of toxicity",
 stem:"Which index of toxicity gives information about the slope of the dose–response curves, and how is it calculated?",
 options:[
  {t:"Therapeutic index, TD50 / ED50", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index compares the toxic and therapeutic doses at the same percentage of animals, so it reflects one point on each curve, not how steep the curves are."},
  {t:"Margin of safety, TD1 / ED99", correct:true, why:"Comparing the dose toxic to 1% with the dose effective in 99% depends on how steep the two curves are."},
  {t:"Margin of exposure, NOAEL / exposure", correct:false, why:"The margin of exposure is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds, not for slope."},
  {t:"Margin of safety, ED99 / TD1", correct:false, why:"The margin of safety divides the toxic dose by the effective dose, not the reverse."}],
 teach:"The margin of safety divides the dose toxic to 1% of animals by the dose effective in 99%. Because it reads the two curves at different percentages, its value depends on how steeply each curve rises, so it carries slope information. The therapeutic index uses the same percentage for the toxic and the therapeutic dose, so it does not. Both are for single doses; the margin of exposure (NOAEL / exposure) is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds.",
 quote:"most of the time, a margin of safety number is developed because we say what's the toxic dose for 1% of the animals over the effective dose of 99% of the animals. That's the margin of safety. And because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.",
 note:"Taught 10/13. Her rule for the therapeutic index: the percentage need not be 50, but the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage; the margin of safety uses two different percentages, which is why it carries slope information.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L13-033", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"moe-repeated", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"objective: calculate common indices of toxicity",
 stem:"The margin of exposure (MOE) is used for:",
 options:[
  {t:"Single doses of a therapeutic compound", correct:false, why:"Single doses use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety."},
  {t:"Repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds", correct:true, why:"The slide marks MOE for repeated exposures and TI and MOS for single doses; her examples were a pollutant and an occupational exposure."},
  {t:"Comparing the slopes of dose–response curves", correct:false, why:"Slope information comes from the margin of safety (TD1 / ED99)."},
  {t:"Finding the dose that kills half the animals", correct:false, why:"That is the LD50, read from the population curve."}],
 teach:"A pollutant or occupational exposure has no therapeutic dose, so the indices that compare toxic with effective doses (TI, MOS) do not fit. The margin of exposure instead divides the NOAEL, the dose at which test animals showed no adverse response, by the exposure actually received; the larger the ratio, the further the exposure is below that no-effect level. The slide marks MOE for repeated exposures and TI and MOS for single doses.",
 quote:"the margin of exposure, this is something that we use truly in toxicology with exposures to something that's not therapeutic, like to, you know, a pollutant, an occupational exposure, OK, and it's also for repeated exposures",
 note:"Taught 10/13. She says NOAEL as 'no observable adverse effect level' (the slide prints 'no observed') and described it as the dose given to the animals at which they had no bad response; she called it the lowest such dose; the assigned reading defines it as the highest dose tested with no significant toxicity (Casarett and Doull ch. 2).",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L13-034", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"target-not-highest-concentration", tags:["smith"], source:"both", core:true, coreWhy:"her quiz item: concentration is highest at the target site (False)",
 stem:"Which statement about the target of a toxic substance is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The target organ is where the concentration is highest", correct:false, why:"Concentration alone does not decide where harm occurs; a more sensitive organ such as the kidney can be harmed at a lower concentration than a less sensitive one such as muscle."},
  {t:"The mechanism of action is the same at each target", correct:false, why:"The mechanism of action at multiple targets may differ."},
  {t:"The target organ may not be where the concentration is highest", correct:true, why:"Harm depends on the target's sensitivity as well as on concentration: the kidney can respond at a lower concentration than muscle, and it is the dose at the target that determines toxicity."},
  {t:"The dose given, not the dose at the target, sets toxicity", correct:false, why:"The dose at the target determines toxicity; even a sensitive organ has its own dose–response curve."}],
 teach:"Whether an organ is harmed depends on how sensitive it is as well as how much compound reaches it. Her example: the kidney can be more sensitive than muscle, so a lower concentration in the kidney can still produce an effect even where muscle holds more. That is why the target organ may not be the site of highest concentration, and why the dose at the target, on that target's own dose–response curve, determines toxicity.",
 quote:"where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive, right?",
 note:"Taught 10/13; her words match the slide. Her example: the kidney may be more sensitive than muscle at a lower concentration, and there is still a dose–response curve at the kidney itself.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- The reading ---------------- */
{id:"L13-035", dupOf:"L13-043", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-source-wwtp", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"slide",
 stem:"In the Rodriguez-Mozaz article, which is the main point of discharge of pharmaceuticals into surface waters?",
 options:[
  {t:"Pharmaceutical manufacturing plants", correct:false, why:"Manufacturing is one source, but the review names treatment-plant effluent as the main discharge point."},
  {t:"Wastewater treatment plant effluents", correct:true, why:"Current treatment does not target pharmaceuticals for removal, so the plants are the main points of discharge."},
  {t:"Drinking-water treatment plant intakes", correct:false, why:"Those plants are where the trace levels are found, not where they enter."},
  {t:"Landfills holding discarded medicines", correct:false, why:"Medicines thrown in the trash are one route, but the drug excreted by patients and passed through treatment plants is the main discharge."}],
 teach:"The assigned review on pharmaceuticals in water carries the five principles. Source: unmetabolized drug and metabolites from human and veterinary use enter waste streams, and wastewater treatment plants, which do not specifically remove pharmaceuticals, are the main discharge points into surface water; unused medicine that is flushed adds to it.",
 note:"On 10/13 she named treatment plants as one of four sources (\"the current mechanisms don't get all the drugs out of it before it goes back into your streams and rivers and lakes\") and said the water is a source for both humans and animals. The 'main discharge' ranking is the article's; she discussed treatment plants as a source and called the reading testable (\"I will later post a key, cause this is testable material\"). L14-024 tests her point.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Discussion of Reading Assignment’; RodriguezMozaz-EHP-2010.pdf p. 1017"},

{id:"L13-036", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lowYield:true,
 concept:"reading-wildlife-effects", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which organisms does the Rodriguez-Mozaz article give as its two examples of wildlife harmed by pharmaceuticals?",
 options:[
  {t:"Humans drinking treated water", correct:false, why:"The review found little evidence of human harm at part-per-trillion levels, and humans are not wildlife."},
  {t:"Vultures and fathead minnows", correct:true, why:"Vultures in India and Pakistan (diclofenac in cattle carcasses) and fathead minnows in a lake dosed with ethinyl estradiol are the two examples."},
  {t:"Cattle and poultry on feedlots", correct:false, why:"Livestock given drugs are a source; the cattle were the source of the diclofenac, not the affected organisms."},
  {t:"Bacteria in lake sediments", correct:false, why:"Antibiotic resistance in sediments is raised as a possible effect, not one of the two examples."}],
 teach:"Target and dose in the reading: vultures feeding on carcasses of diclofenac-treated cattle died of renal failure because they could not excrete the drug, and fathead minnows in a lake deliberately dosed with low levels of ethinyl estradiol showed cellular and tissue responses, then a population collapse by the second year. Humans, exposed at part-per-trillion levels in drinking water, receive far less than a therapeutic dose over a lifetime, so ecosystems appear more at risk than people.",
 note:"Not discussed in class: on 10/13 she did not mention the vultures, diclofenac, fathead minnows or ethinyl estradiol. Her wildlife example was frogs, fish and alligators turning more female where estrogen levels are higher (L14-030). Kept low yield; the article text keys it.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Discussion of Reading Assignment’; RodriguezMozaz-EHP-2010.pdf pp. 1017–1018"},

/* ---------------- Her practice quiz 1 (verbatim, with her keys); shown on the "Quizzes (verbatim)" card (QZ3, sub quiz1) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-037", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"adverse-despite-low-concentration", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A compound with a half-life that allows it to be cleared from the body before the next exposure may still result in chronic adverse effects",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:true, why:"Her key. Compound C on the frequency figure never reaches the toxic blood concentration, yet she listed allergy, another target, synergy with other drugs and injection-site damage as ways it can still harm."},
  {t:"False", correct:false, why:"Her key is True: clearance between doses rules out accumulation, not every adverse response."}],
 teach:"Clearing between exposures only stops a compound from building up to a toxic blood concentration. Harm that does not depend on blood concentration can still occur with repeated use: an allergy needs very little drug, the compound may act at a different target from the one the curve describes, it can interact with other drugs (she said \"synergize\") and add to their adverse effects, and an injection can damage the injection site. So compound C is \"not necessarily all good to go.\"",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz; the key is the one she released. Same point as L13-016.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 1"},

{id:"L13-038", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"exposure-durations", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Chronic exposure is considered to be repeated exposure for what length of time?",
 options:[
  {t:"Less than 24 hours", correct:false, why:"Acute."},
  {t:"1-3 months", correct:false, why:"Subchronic."},
  {t:"More than three months", correct:true, why:"Her key: chronic is more than 3 months, up to years."},
  {t:"Less than a month", correct:false, why:"Subacute."}],
 teach:"Animal studies define exposure by how long the animals are dosed: acute under 24 hours, subacute under 1 month, subchronic 1 to 3 months, and chronic more than 3 months up to years. Because the durations are fixed in advance, the study knows how long the animals were exposed and can tie the effects to that length. Human exposures are sorted on the same scale, though a person often cannot say how long a workplace or environmental exposure has gone on.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same table as L13-010.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 2"},

{id:"L13-039", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-mos-moe", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"What value derived from a dose-response curve has the following formula: X = Toxic Dose/Therapeutic Dose",
 options:[
  {t:"Threshold Dose", correct:false, why:"The threshold is read from the curve, not calculated as a ratio."},
  {t:"Margin of Safety", correct:false, why:"MOS = TD1 / ED99: two different percentages."},
  {t:"Margin of Exposure", correct:false, why:"MOE = NOAEL / exposure."},
  {t:"Therapeutic Index", correct:true, why:"Her key: TI = toxic dose / therapeutic dose, with the same percentage in numerator and denominator."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 3. The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose at the same percentage of the population; it carries no slope information, which is what separates it from the margin of safety.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-032.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 3"},

{id:"L13-040", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"admet-t", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"What does ADME stand for?",
 options:[
  {t:"Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion", correct:true, why:"Her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer."},
  {t:"Administration, Distribution, Metabolism, Elimination", correct:false, why:"The A is absorption, not administration."},
  {t:"Absorption, Dose, Mechanism, Exposure", correct:false, why:"Dose, mechanism and exposure are other principles, not the letters of ADME."},
  {t:"Absorption, Distribution, Mechanism, Excretion", correct:false, why:"The M is metabolism."}],
 teach:"ADME follows the path of anything that enters the body: it is absorbed (or injected by IV), distributed through the body, likely metabolized, then excreted. Toxicology adds a T for toxicity. The step it watches most is metabolism, because metabolism can turn a compound into something nastier (bioactivation, as acetaminophen to NAPQI) or into something less harmful and easier to eliminate (detoxification).",
 note:"On the quiz this was a free-response question, not multiple choice; the exam may ask for the expansion written out. Options added here for the drill.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 4"},

{id:"L13-041", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"toxic-target-differs", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The mechanisms of a drug’s toxicity is always the same as the mechanism of its efficacy.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False: the toxic target can differ from the therapeutic target."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. The target of something toxic is not always the same as its therapeutic target."}],
 teach:"A drug's benefit and its harm can come from different places in the body by different routes. Acetaminophen relieves soreness, but its liver toxicity comes from NAPQI, a metabolite made by CYP2E1 when the phase 2 enzymes cannot handle the dose. The slide adds that a compound can have multiple targets with a different mechanism at each, so \"always the same\" is false.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-005.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 5"},

{id:"L13-042", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"target-not-highest-concentration", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The concentration of a medication in the body is always highest at its target site.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False. The site with the most drug need not be the target: the kidney can respond at a lower concentration than muscle, so harm may occur where the concentration is lower."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. The target organ may not be the site of highest concentration; the dose at the target determines toxicity."}],
 teach:"Practice quiz 1, question 6: the target-and-mechanism slide she covered on 10/13. Where a compound has its adverse effect may not be where its concentration is highest; the kidney can be more sensitive than muscle at a lower concentration.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. Same point as L13-034.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 6"},

{id:"L13-043", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-source-human-use", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The article by Rodriguez-Mozaz discusses the fact that pharmaceuticals have been detected in ground water, surface water, and drinking water. What is most likely the greatest source of pharmaceuticals in those waters?",
 options:[
  {t:"Unused medications thrown in the trash leaching out of landfills.", correct:false, why:"Only leftover medicine goes in the trash; the far larger amount is what patients take, which is excreted, metabolized and unmetabolized, into sewage every day."},
  {t:"Pharmaceutical company manufacturing processes.", correct:false, why:"Manufacturing contributes, but the drug consumed by people everywhere is excreted into waste streams that treatment plants do not fully clean, and that outweighs it."},
  {t:"Patients flushing unused medications down the toilet.", correct:false, why:"Flushing is improper disposal and a real source, but it involves only unused medicine; normal use sends excreted drug and metabolites into sewage from every patient taking it."},
  {t:"Consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams", correct:true, why:"Her key: the drug and its metabolites that people excrete after normal use reach the waste stream and the treatment plants."}],
 teach:"Every person who takes a medication excretes the drug and its metabolites in urine and feces, so ordinary use feeds the waste stream from a whole community. That sewage reaches wastewater treatment plants, which do not remove all the drugs before the water returns to streams, rivers and lakes. Flushing, landfills and manufacturing add to it, but they involve only part of the drug supply, so human consumption is the greatest source.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key. L13-035 and L14-024 test the same source principle; this wording is hers.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 7"},

{id:"L13-044", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-human-dose-below-therapeutic", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"The dose of pharmaceuticals that people are exposed to in drinking water exceeds the daily dose of that medication when used therapeutically.",
 options:[
  {t:"True", correct:false, why:"Her key is False. Drinking-water levels are in parts per trillion, which does not reach a daily effective dose; the worry is continuous exposure of wildlife and more sensitive people, not a dose above therapeutic."},
  {t:"False", correct:true, why:"Her key. Drinking-water levels are far below a therapeutic daily dose."}],
 teach:"Pharmaceuticals in drinking water are measured in parts per trillion, an amount that does not bring a person up to a daily effective dose. Concern therefore shifts to those exposed continuously or more sensitive: wildlife living in the water, which may not process drugs as humans do, and people who are immunocompromised, very young or exposed in utero.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 8"},

{id:"L13-045", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reading-conclusion", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Which is the best statement that describes the conclusion the authors made in this article?",
 options:[
  {t:"Pharmaceuticals in water are not adversely affecting aquatic wildlife.", correct:false, why:"The article's wildlife examples show adverse effects."},
  {t:"Pharmaceuticals in waters are adversely affecting human health.", correct:false, why:"The authors did not conclude that; human exposure is far below therapeutic doses."},
  {t:"No answers are correct", correct:false, why:"The authors did reach a conclusion: the data on human health effects are not yet enough to decide, which is the \"more data\" option."},
  {t:"More data is needed to determine if pharmaceuticals in water are adversely affecting human health.", correct:true, why:"Her key: the authors call for more data on human health effects."}],
 teach:"The authors separate what is known from what is not. For wildlife, which live in the water and are exposed continuously, adverse effects are documented. For people, drinking-water levels are in parts per trillion, below a daily effective dose, and studies do not exist for all the metabolites, so harm to human health can be neither shown nor ruled out. Hence the call for more data.",
 note:"Verbatim from her practice quiz with her key.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 9"},

{id:"L13-046", lecture:"QZ3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"QZ3", sub:"quiz1", skill:"recall",
 concept:"five-principles", tags:["smith","quiz","verbatim"], source:"slide",
 stem:"List the five principles of toxicology:",
 options:[
  {t:"Source; Exposure; ADMET; Dose; Target and Mechanism of Action", correct:true, why:"Her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer."},
  {t:"Source; Route; Metabolism; Dose; Receptor", correct:false, why:"Route is part of exposure and metabolism part of ADMET; receptor is not the principle's name."},
  {t:"Toxin; Toxicant; Xenobiotic; Poison; Dose", correct:false, why:"Those are the definitions, not the principles."},
  {t:"Source; Exposure; Half-life; LD50; Threshold", correct:false, why:"Half-life, LD50 and threshold are values inside the exposure and dose principles."}],
 teach:"The five principles are source, exposure, ADMET, dose, and target and mechanism of action. Each asks one question about a poison: where it comes from, how people or critters are exposed, what its kinetics are (with the T for toxicity), whether the exposure is high enough to cause a problem, and where it goes in the body and how it causes harm. Route, metabolism, half-life, LD50 and threshold sit inside these principles rather than beside them.",
 note:"On the quiz this was a free-response list; the exam may ask for it written out. Options added here for the drill.",
 cite:"Smith_Practice_Quiz_1_-_Principles_of_Toxicology.pdf question 10"},
/* ---------------- The coral snake on 'What is Toxic?' (her spoken question, 10/9) ---------------- */
{id:"L13-047", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"apply",
 concept:"coral-snake-rhyme", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A snake has red, yellow and black bands, and its red bands touch its yellow bands. What is it most likely to be?",
 options:[
  {t:"A king snake", correct:false, why:"On the king snake the red touches the black: \"Red and black, friend of Jack.\" It is not poisonous."},
  {t:"A coral snake", correct:true, why:"\"Red and yellow kills a fellow\": red touching yellow marks the venomous coral snake."},
  {t:"A rattlesnake", correct:false, why:"The rattlesnake is one of the fanged snakes she contrasted with the coral snake; the banding rhyme is about the coral snake and its look-alike, the king snake."},
  {t:"A water moccasin", correct:false, why:"The water moccasin is another fanged Texas snake; the banding rhyme is about the coral snake and its look-alike, the king snake."}],
 teach:"The coral snake and the king snake both carry red, yellow and black bands, in a different order. On the coral snake the red touches the yellow (\"Red and yellow kills a fellow\"); on the king snake the red touches the black (\"Red and black, friend of Jack\"), and it is not poisonous: people keep king snakes as pets. The coral snake is very venomous; its venom carries a neurotoxin. It lives in Texas, especially East Texas.",
 quote:"Coral snake, how do you know that? Red and y'all, everybody know that poem. Red and yellow kills a fellow, so if the red touches yellow, it's poisonous, OK? Red and black, friend of Jack.",
 note:"She showed the snakes as a fun opener (\"I just like to know about poisonous animals and plants. I think it's fun.\") before turning to medications; on 10/19 she said everything said in class is testable.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘What is Toxic?’; transcript 10/9"},
{id:"L13-048", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"defs", skill:"recall",
 concept:"coral-snake-venom", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript", dupOf:"L13-047",
 stem:"What kind of toxin does the coral snake's venom carry?",
 options:[
  {t:"A neurotoxin", correct:true, why:"Her words: \"So this is a coral snake, very venomous, they have a neurotoxin.\""},
  {t:"A hepatotoxin", correct:false, why:"A liver toxin was her acetaminophen example (NAPQI); for the coral snake she named a neurotoxin."},
  {t:"A carcinogen", correct:false, why:"A snakebite is a single, immediate exposure; she named a neurotoxin."},
  {t:"A toxicant", correct:false, why:"Venom is made by the snake, so it is a toxin; a toxicant comes from human activity."}],
 teach:"The coral snake is very venomous, and its venom carries a neurotoxin. Because the snake makes it, the venom is a toxin rather than a toxicant. It has a small head and is not a viper, so instead of striking with fangs like a rattlesnake it bites and chews, often between fingers or toes, to inject the venom. Its look-alike, the king snake (red touching black), is not poisonous.",
 quote:"So this is a coral snake, very venomous, they have a neurotoxin. They are here in Texas, um, they're in East Texas, especially",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘What is Toxic?’; transcript 10/9"},
{id:"L13-049", lecture:"L13", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L13", sub:"freq", skill:"figure",
 concept:"fig2-4-read-b-dose", tags:["smith"], source:"both", simg:"smith-fig2-4", simgAlt:"Casarett and Doull Figure 2-4: concentration at the target site over time for compounds A, B and C, after one large dose (left) and after repeated smaller doses (right), with the toxic range shaded.",
 stem:"In the repeated-dose panel, at about which dose does compound B first reach the toxic range?",
 options:[
  {t:"The 1st dose", correct:false, why:"The first repeated dose is only one quarter of the single dose and sits well below the toxic range for every compound."},
  {t:"The 2nd dose", correct:false, why:"After the 2nd dose B's peak is still below the shaded toxic range; each dose only adds a little to what is left of the last."},
  {t:"The 3rd dose", correct:false, why:"That is compound A, which barely declines between doses and enters the toxic range at about the 3rd dose."},
  {t:"The 4th or 5th dose", correct:true, why:"B's peaks climb slowly because about half is cleared between doses; extending its line, it enters the toxic range at about the 4th dose and is in it by the 5th."}],
 teach:"Read each compound's peaks across the repeated doses. A barely declines between doses, so it stacks and is toxic by about the 3rd dose. B, with a half-life about equal to the dosing interval, loses roughly half between doses, so its peaks rise more slowly and reach the toxic range at about the 4th or 5th dose. C is cleared before the next dose and never builds up.",
 quote:"When does compound B start causing a problem? How many doses? Maybe 4, they didn't draw this very well, right, but if you extrapolate, probably 4 is looking like this, right? Maybe 4, you're in the toxic zone. 5 for sure, you're in the toxic zone, right, for B.",
 note:"The figure stops before B's line clearly enters the toxic range; she extrapolated: \"Maybe 4 ... 5 for sure\". The caption gives B a half-life approximately equal to the dosing frequency.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Exposure: Frequency’ (C&D Fig 2-4 and caption); transcript 10/9"}

);
